## I. THE ENGINEERING CONTEXT THAT CREATES THIS PROFESSION

The history of construction engineering is a history of expanding the boundary of what is physically buildable. Aqueducts extended that boundary across valleys. Suspension bridges extended it across water. Pressurized skyscrapers extended it into thin air. Each extension required new engineering disciplines, new failure theories, and new methods of quality assurance.

The Moon extends that boundary into a regime where the three foundational assumptions of construction engineering — a human can get to the structure, the structure's environment is modeled with sufficient fidelity, and a qualified inspector can assess the work — are systematically unavailable.

A human cannot reach a robot installing a pressure boundary element during an active construction sequence in an unpressurized zone at −130°C. The regolith's subsurface structure is unknown at meter-scale precision, and the robot's model of it is derived from orbital remote sensing and a handful of Apollo core samples taken at a different latitude and geological context. And the "inspector" is a sensor array on a machine operating in an environment where dust accumulation has been degrading its optical components at a rate the maintenance schedule did not anticipate.

This is not a construction engineering problem with lunar trimmings. It is a categorically different engineering problem, requiring a profession that does not yet exist.

The Extraterrestrial Robotic Construction Engineer is that profession. They design, deploy, operate, and continuously validate autonomous and semi-autonomous robotic construction systems in environments where human presence is intermittent at best, where the physical environment diverges from models at operationally critical scales, and where the consequences of construction failure are not contractual and financial but immediately and permanently life-threatening.

Three active research streams have converged to make this profession simultaneously urgent and technically feasible. NASA's Autonomous Robotic Assembly of Lunar Surface Infrastructure (ARALI) project is developing multi-agent robotic systems with advanced path-planning and control algorithms for modular habitat, satellite, and telescope assembly. The construction research literature has organized the primary equipment categories: excavation and transport systems (RASSOR-class), mobile 3D printing platforms (regolith-based sintered deposition), and robotic masonry assemblers (precast regolith block placement and bonding). And digital twin research — progressed to physics-based simulation of robotic equipment-regolith interaction — is solving previously unsolvable problems in mechanical design and autonomous navigation for lunar surface systems.

The profession emerges at the intersection of all three.

