## II. WHAT THIS PROFESSION ACTUALLY DOES

The Extraterrestrial Robotic Construction Engineer designs and operates multi-class autonomous robotic construction systems on the lunar surface to produce structures whose as-built configuration is verifiably safe, whose construction sequence is resilient to environmental deviation and mechanical degradation, and whose completion does not require continuous human presence in the hazardous zone.

This definition contains four technical commitments that each require independent engineering development.

**Multi-class robotic systems.** Not a single robot or a homogeneous swarm, but a coordinated fleet of fundamentally different machine types — excavators, printers, assemblers, surveyors, transporters — each with distinct failure modes, maintenance requirements, and decision authorities. The coordination of dissimilar autonomous agents across a shared construction environment, where each agent's local actions alter the environment for all others, is the central algorithmic challenge of the profession.

**Verifiably safe as-built configuration.** The construction process produces not only a physical structure but an accompanying computational proof that the as-built configuration satisfies the structural design envelope. This proof is generated continuously, updated at every construction checkpoint, and must remain valid at mission completion. If it cannot be generated — if accumulated deviation between as-designed and as-built parameters has exited the structural tolerance envelope — construction halts. No exception.

**Resilient construction sequence.** The construction plan is not a static schedule executed in order. It is a dependency graph with defined substitution protocols, rerouting logic, and graceful degradation modes. When a robot fails, when regolith behaves unexpectedly, when a printed section fails quality verification — the sequence adapts not by improvising, but by executing one of a pre-computed set of contingency branches that have been validated for structural integrity before construction begins.

**Without requiring continuous human presence in the hazardous zone.** Humans are not absent from this system. They are present at the control interface, available for Level 3 and Level 4 decisions, and available for EVA intervention in specific contingency scenarios. But the construction sequence must be able to run for extended periods — hours, potentially days — without requiring a human to physically enter the construction zone, because the environment that kills robotic actuators slowly is capable of killing humans in minutes.

