## III. THE UNSTRUCTURED ENVIRONMENT PROBLEM: WHY EARTH CONSTRUCTION ENGINEERING FAILS ON THE MOON

Earth construction engineering is built on a substrate of reducible uncertainty. The soil at a construction site can be sampled at high resolution before work begins. The structural behavior of materials is governed by well-characterized mechanics tested over centuries of practice. Thermal and moisture conditions are bounded and cyclically predictable within a range the engineering standards were designed for. And when something unexpected is encountered — a buried boulder, an unexpected soil layer, a material defect — a qualified engineer walks to the site, looks at it, and makes a decision.

None of these conditions hold on the Moon.

The regolith's mechanical behavior — cohesion, compaction, bearing capacity — varies at sub-meter scales in ways that cannot be fully resolved from orbital sensing. The thermal environment imposes a range of 300 degrees Celsius across a 29.5-day cycle, producing differential thermal expansion in structural elements and mechanical fatigue in robot actuators that has no direct Earth analog. Vacuum-ultraviolet radiation degrades polymer seals, lubricants, and optical components on timescales that laboratory testing approximates but does not fully replicate. And the fine regolith — particles sized 10–100 microns, electrostatically charged by solar wind, mechanically abrasive enough to sand-blast metal surfaces — infiltrates every joint, seal, and bearing surface of every robot operating on the surface.

The consequence is that every construction robot operates in an environment its model does not fully describe, and degrades at a rate that no off-Earth service history has calibrated. The profession must design for this: not by eliminating the uncertainty, but by building construction systems that detect, quantify, and adapt to it continuously.

The five failure modes of this profession flow directly from the five ways this unstructured environment defeats conventional construction engineering.

