## IV. FAILURE MODE 1 — THE PHYSICAL REALITY GAP AND THE ADAPTIVE SITE MODEL

The construction plan is built on a model. The model is built from orbital remote sensing, Earth-based telescope data, seismic surveys from prior robotic precursors, and extrapolation from Apollo-era core samples collected at different latitudes and geological contexts. The model specifies regolith bearing capacity, sub-surface layering, boulder probability density, ice content at depth, and surface slope at the construction site.

The model is wrong. Not catastrophically wrong — the regolith at the construction site will broadly resemble the model's prediction. But operationally wrong at critical scales: the bearing capacity under a habitat foundation pad may be 20% lower than predicted due to a localized ancient microimpact that compacted a thin layer differently. A RASSOR excavator hitting that layer at the expected depth and pressure will over-excavate in a way that undermines the foundation geometry. A 3D printing platform tracking the surface slope model will deposit material at an angle that produces a structurally compromised wall section.

Each individual divergence from the model may be small. But construction sequences are dependency graphs. A foundation whose geometry deviates from design by 8 centimeters propagates geometric error into every structural element that connects to it.

The conventional response — send an engineer to look at it — is not available. The architectural response must be built into the system before the first robot operates.

### Architectural Solution: The Adaptive Site Model with Physical Reality Gap Protocol

The Adaptive Site Model is not a static pre-mission document. It is a probabilistic, continuously updated computational model of the construction site, initialized from pre-mission survey data and updated in real time from sensor returns of every robot operating on the site.

The model maintains, for each 10cm × 10cm × 10cm voxel of the construction volume, a probability distribution over its physical properties: bearing capacity, composition, moisture content, compaction state. Before a robot begins any operation — excavation, printing, assembly, transport — it queries the Adaptive Site Model for the target volume and receives not a single value but a distribution: bearing capacity is modeled as N(μ, σ²) based on current evidence.

If σ is small (evidence is consistent), the robot proceeds with planned parameters.

If σ exceeds an alert threshold σ_alert, the robot executes a slow-approach protocol: reduced force, reduced speed, continuous force/torque sensor logging, with each sensor return fed back to the Adaptive Site Model to narrow the distribution before the critical phase of the operation.

If any sensor return during slow-approach produces a posterior update placing a critical property outside the structural design envelope, the robot suspends the operation and triggers a Physical Reality Gap event.

**Physical Reality Gap Classification**

| Gap Class | Criterion | Response |
|---|---|---|
| Class I: Nominal | σ ≤ σ_alert; posterior within design envelope | Robot proceeds at standard parameters; Adaptive Site Model updated |
| Class II: Monitored | σ > σ_alert; posterior within design envelope | Robot proceeds on slow-approach; human notification only |
| Class III: Conditional | Posterior approaches design envelope boundary | Operation suspended; contingency branch activated; human review within 4 hours |
| Class IV: Critical | Posterior exits design envelope | Operation suspended; structural checkpoint triggered; human Level 3 decision required before resumption |

The Physical Reality Gap Protocol is not a safety check on individual operations. It is the live interface between the construction plan and the physical world. The plan executes on the model. The model executes on the site. When the site contradicts the model, the protocol mediates the contradiction before it becomes a structural defect.

### Mathematical Formulation: Structural Integrity Confidence Index

The Physical Reality Gap Protocol operates in parallel with the construction sequence's primary quality assurance instrument: the Structural Integrity Confidence Index (SICI).

At each construction checkpoint k, the as-built parameter vector B_k ∈ ℝⁿ is reconstructed from photogrammetric survey, LIDAR point cloud registration, and embedded sensor telemetry. A reduced-order finite element model of the structure in its current as-built state is solved under the design load cases, producing the principal stress field σ(B_k) and, at each structural element e, the safety factor:

$$F_e(k) = \frac{\sigma_{\text{yield},e}}{\sigma_e(B_k)}$$

where σ_yield,e is the yield strength of element e's material and σ_e(B_k) is the maximum principal stress in that element under the design load combination.

The SICI at checkpoint k is:

$$\text{SICI}(k) = \frac{F_{\min}(k) - F_{\text{crit}}}{F_{\text{design}} - F_{\text{crit}}}$$

where F_min(k) = min_e F_e(k) is the minimum safety factor across all structural elements, F_crit is the minimum permissible safety factor below which the structure is unsafe, and F_design is the target safety factor in the original structural design.

**Interpretation:**

SICI > 1: As-built structure meets or exceeds the design safety target. Construction proceeds.
0 < SICI ≤ 1: As-built structure is safe but degraded from design intent. Yellow flag: contingency branch evaluation required before the next structural operation.
SICI ≤ 0: Structural integrity cannot be certified. Mandatory construction halt. Human Level 4 decision required.

The SICI is the primary public instrument of construction progress. It is not a background metric visible only to engineers — it is displayed in the control center continuously, the way atmospheric CO₂ concentration is displayed in the habitat. When the SICI is green, construction proceeds. When it turns yellow, the system evaluates which contingency branch best recovers the structural safety margin. When it turns red, no robot executes a structural operation until a human has reviewed the as-built model, authorized a redesign or repair protocol, and the SICI has been restored to the green range.

