## V. FAILURE MODE 2 — MULTI-AGENT COORDINATION CASCADE AND THE SWARM COHERENCE PROTOCOL

A habitat construction sequence involving twenty simultaneous robots — four RASSOR-class excavators, six mobile printing platforms, seven robotic assemblers, three survey drones — produces a dependency graph with hundreds of inter-agent dependencies active at any moment. Assembler A cannot place column C until Printer P has completed and cured wall section W. Excavator E must complete trench T before Transport robot Tr can deliver material M to Printer P's staging area. Survey drone D must complete geometric verification of foundation F before Assembler A is authorized to begin.

Under 1.3 seconds of Earth-to-Moon latency, and with robot-to-robot mesh network latency that may reach 50–200 milliseconds under lunar surface radio propagation conditions, a coordination cascade — where one robot's delay or failure propagates through the dependency graph and produces conflicting actions by otherwise-uncoordinated robots — is a constant operational threat.

The specific failure mode is not robot-to-robot collision, which proximity sensors and reactive collision avoidance address at the individual level. It is structural sequence violation: a structural operation executed at the wrong time, producing an as-built configuration that cannot be corrected without demolition. Demolition on the Moon is not a cost-plus change order. It is weeks of lost mission time and potentially unrecoverable structural material.

### Architectural Solution: Hierarchical Swarm Coherence Protocol

The Swarm Coherence Protocol operates on three tiers, each handling a distinct temporal and spatial scale of coordination.

**Tier 1 — Reactive Layer (millisecond-to-second scale, individual robot)**

Each robot maintains continuous local awareness of its immediate environment — 5-meter collision radius, task boundary markers, safety exclusion zones — using onboard sensors. Tier 1 decisions are made autonomously, without network communication. A robot that detects an obstacle in its path stops. A robot that detects it is outside its task boundary stops. No network latency affects Tier 1 safety responses.

**Tier 2 — Assembly Graph Manager (second-to-minute scale, fleet-wide)**

A dedicated computation agent — not a physical robot, but a software process running on the site's main computing cluster — maintains the current state of the construction dependency graph. Every robot reports its task state (in-progress, complete, suspended, faulted) at regular intervals. The Assembly Graph Manager holds the authority to authorize the next task in a sequence, reroute a task to a different robot when a planned robot is unavailable, and flag sequence violations before they are executed.

Critically, the Assembly Graph Manager does not instruct robots in real time. It issues authorizations. A robot does not begin its next task because its current task is complete. It begins because the Assembly Graph Manager has authorized the start, based on verification that all predecessor tasks in the dependency graph are confirmed complete and the structural sequence constraint is satisfied.

This distinction eliminates the category of failure where a robot begins the next task before its predecessor is actually complete — the most common source of structural sequence violations in multi-agent construction systems.

**Tier 3 — Structural Checkpoint Authority (hour-to-day scale, human oversight)**

At defined points in the construction sequence — foundation completion, pressure boundary closure, primary structural frame completion — the Assembly Graph Manager issues a structural checkpoint pause. All robots halt. The SICI is computed for the structure's current as-built state. A photogrammetric survey is conducted by the survey drones. The checkpoint verification package — SICI value, as-built point cloud, deviation map from design geometry — is transmitted to the human operator for Level 3 review.

The human confirms, requests additional verification, or authorizes a contingency branch. Only after human authorization does the Assembly Graph Manager release the next phase of the construction sequence.

The three tiers are independent in their failure modes. A Tier 1 failure (individual robot sensor failure) does not propagate to Tier 2. A Tier 2 failure (Assembly Graph Manager software fault) does not propagate to the physical structure, because all robots default to task suspension on loss of Assembly Graph Manager authorization signal. A Tier 3 checkpoint failure (human unavailable for review) causes construction to pause — not to proceed without authorization.

### Swarm Coherence Index

The Swarm Coherence Index (SwCI) provides a continuous quantitative measure of multi-agent coordination health.

Let G_t = (V_t, E_t) be the directed dependency graph at time t, where V_t is the set of pending and active tasks and E_t is the set of dependency edges (edge (u,v) means task v cannot begin until task u is complete). Let K_t ⊆ V_t be the set of tasks currently in a state inconsistent with the graph's dependency constraints — tasks that have been authorized or begun before their predecessors completed.

$$\text{SwCI}(t) = 1 - \frac{|K_t|}{|V_t|}$$

SwCI = 1: All active tasks are consistent with the dependency graph. Full coordination health.
SwCI < 0.95: One or more tasks executing in violation of dependency constraints. Yellow flag: Assembly Graph Manager initiates rerouting analysis.
SwCI < 0.90: Multiple violations. All robots suspended. Human notification required.
SwCI < 0.85: Critical multi-agent coordination failure. All robots execute safe-state protocols. Human Level 3 decision required before any robot resumes operation.

