## VII. FAILURE MODE 4 — AUTONOMOUS MISSION CREEP AND THE STRUCTURAL AUTHORITY BOUNDARY

A robotic assembler is placing a pressure boundary panel. Its sensor returns indicate the mating surface geometry doesn't match the design file — the 3D-printed wall section it's connecting to has a surface flatness deviation of 2.3mm, against a design tolerance of 1.0mm. The assembler has two options: flag the deviation and suspend the task, or proceed with a locally computed compensation strategy — adjusting the sealant bead width and fastener torque to accommodate the mismatch.

The locally computed compensation strategy is, in isolation, mechanically reasonable. The adjusted parameters will produce an adequate seal. The assembler executes it and logs it as a local autonomous adjustment.

Forty-two such adjustments occur across the habitat's pressure boundary closure sequence — each locally reasonable, each logged, none individually exceeding a threshold that would trigger human review. The cumulative effect is a pressure boundary whose sealant system has been executed at non-nominal parameters at 42 points, with an as-built configuration that has never been analyzed as a system. The structure passes its initial pressurization test. Eighteen months later, at a pressure excursion associated with a thermal event, three of those adjusted sealant points fail simultaneously.

Autonomous mission creep — the accumulation of locally authorized but globally unreviewed autonomous adjustments — is the most insidious failure mode in multi-agent autonomous construction. It produces no single dramatic failure event. It produces a slow, invisible divergence between the structure the colony depends on and the structure that was actually designed and verified.

### Architectural Solution: Structural Authority Boundary Protocol

The Structural Authority Boundary Protocol is a formal taxonomy of every construction parameter, specifying the authority level required to adjust it from its design value.

**Category A — Operational Parameters**

Parameters governing how a robot performs a task, not what it produces: speed, approach trajectory, tool selection, intermediate positioning. Category A parameters may be autonomously adjusted by individual robots within defined ranges to optimize execution efficiency. No logging requirement beyond standard operational telemetry.

**Category B — Quality Parameters**

Parameters governing the dimensional and material quality of the construction output, within the tolerance bands specified in the design documents: panel flatness within ±50% of design tolerance, sealant bead width within ±15% of specification, fastener torque within ±10% of specification. Category B adjustments may be autonomously executed but require mandatory logging with deviation magnitude and compensating action — and trigger automatic SICI recomputation before any dependent task is authorized.

**Category C — Structural Parameters**

Parameters governing the structural integrity and pressure boundary function of the construction output. Any parameter whose deviation from design would require structural re-analysis to certify safety. Pressure boundary sealant parameters beyond Category B tolerance. Foundation geometry deviation beyond ±5mm. Primary structural connection orientation beyond ±0.5°. Load-path element thickness beyond ±3% of design. Category C deviations are not autonomous decisions. They are full stops. The robot suspends the task. The deviation is transmitted to the human operator. The human reviews the as-built condition, requests structural analysis if needed, and issues a Level 3 decision: proceed with a designed remediation, attempt re-execution of the task, or escalate for design modification. No robot may execute a Category C adjustment on autonomous authority. No exception.

The protocol also defines what happens when a robot cannot complete a task without a Category C deviation — when the physical reality gap is large enough that design-nominal execution is not possible. In this case, the contingency branch is activated: the Assembly Graph Manager reroutes to a pre-validated alternative construction sequence, or initiates a Class III/IV Physical Reality Gap event. The robot does not improvise.

