## VIII. FAILURE MODE 5 — HUMAN-ROBOT INTERFACE BREAKDOWN AND THE EMERGENCY OVERRIDE ARCHITECTURE

The Extraterrestrial Robotic Construction Engineer is not present in the construction zone. They are at a control interface: a workstation with telemetry dashboards, 3D terrain and structure models, robot status displays, and video feeds from site cameras and robot-mounted sensors. The physical separation between operator and robots is measured in hundreds of meters, sometimes kilometers.

Under normal operations, this distance is managed. The control interface provides adequate situational awareness. Decisions are deliberate. Latency to individual robots is measured in milliseconds over the site's mesh network.

Under emergency conditions — a structural failure in progress, a robot behaving unexpectedly near completed work, a simultaneous multi-robot fault — the interface is overwhelmed. The operator receives a simultaneous cascade of alerts. The 3D terrain model is out of date relative to the failure event. The robot they most urgently need to stop is not the one whose feed is currently on screen. The override command they need is three menus deep in an interface designed for deliberate use.

In the time it takes to navigate those three menus, the robot has executed two more actions.

The Emergency Override Architecture is not a feature of the control interface. It is a separate, physically independent, parallel-channel control system designed specifically for the conditions under which the primary interface fails its operator.

### Architectural Solution: Emergency Override Architecture with Deadman Protocol

The Emergency Override Architecture has three mandatory properties.

**Physical Independence.** The emergency override channel uses a dedicated radio link to each robot on the site — physically separate from the primary operational data network, with an independent power supply, operating on a frequency band reserved for override commands only. A fault in the primary operational network that causes a coordination failure — a plausible precipitating event for an emergency — does not impair the emergency override channel. The override channel carries exactly one type of message: suspend, targeted to a specific robot or to all robots simultaneously.

**Robot-Specific and Fleet-Wide Override.** The emergency override interface provides two modes of action, accessible from a physical switch — not a software menu. Mode 1 suspends a specific robot by designation code. Mode 2 suspends the entire fleet simultaneously. Both actions execute in one physical gesture. Both take effect within site mesh network latency — not Earth-Moon latency. The override command bypasses the Assembly Graph Manager entirely and executes directly at the individual robot's motion controller.

**Minimum Viable Situational Awareness Display.** Alongside the full-featured primary control interface, a secondary display — ruggedized, with a separate power supply — provides a continuously updated, deliberately simplified representation of the construction site: each robot's position, heading, task state, and proximity to structural elements and other robots. This display requires no interaction. It updates automatically. It is always visible, even when the operator is focused on a specific robot's detailed feed in the primary interface.

**Deadman Protocol.** For operations where a robot is executing a task in close proximity to a structural element whose failure would be catastrophic — pressure boundary installation, foundation grouting, primary structural connection — the operator must hold a physical deadman switch continuously depressed. If the operator releases the switch, the relevant robot suspends its task immediately, regardless of the stage of execution. This protocol eliminates the scenario in which a robot continues a critical structural operation with no human oversight — not because of a software failure, but because the human has left the interface.

Deadman Protocol applicability is specified in the Structural Authority Boundary: it is mandatory for all Category C parameter operations, all pressure boundary tasks, and all foundation grouting operations.

