## IX. THE THREE DESIGN LAYERS — CONSTRUCTION EDITION

These five failure modes integrate into three design layers: specifications locked before the first robot operates, protocols governing day-to-day construction operations, and infrastructure built into the site the way the power grid is built into the habitat.

**Layer 1 — Structural Construction Architecture**
*(Designed and locked before any robot operates on the surface)*

Five mandatory specifications define the pre-operational design layer.

*Construction Dependency Graph.* The complete task dependency graph for the full construction sequence — every task, every dependency, every contingency branch — validated for structural integrity at each branch by structural analysis before construction begins. No task is added to the graph during construction without dependency graph validation and SICI projection.

*Structural Authority Boundary Taxonomy.* The full classification of every construction parameter as Category A, B, or C, applied to every robot class operating on the site. This document carries constitutional status: it is co-signed by the construction lead, the structural engineer, and the mission commander.

*Predictive Degradation Threshold Registry.* The δ₁ and δ₂ failure probability thresholds for every monitored component in every robot class, calibrated to the site's specific operational environment (dust composition, thermal profile, radiation environment) from pre-mission characterization data. Thresholds are established before deployment and updated by the Fleet-Wide Degradation Pattern Learning subsystem during operations.

*SICI Checkpoint Schedule.* The defined checkpoints in the construction sequence at which SICI is computed and human Level 3 review is required before the sequence advances. Checkpoint frequency is highest at the stages of highest structural consequence: foundation completion, primary structural frame, pressure boundary closure.

*Emergency Override Architecture Specification.* The technical specifications for the independent override channel — frequency allocation, power system, antenna placement — and the Deadman Protocol applicability map: every task class for which the Deadman Protocol is mandatory, and the specific structural elements that define "catastrophic failure proximity."

**Layer 2 — Behavioral Protocol Design**
*(Operational specifications for human-robot-system interaction)*

Four mandatory behavioral specifications govern construction operations.

*Physical Reality Gap Escalation Protocol.* The defined response sequence for each Gap Class (I through IV), including the time limits for human response to Class III and IV events, and the safe-state default for each robot class when a Gap event suspends its task.

*Structural Checkpoint Review Protocol.* The defined content of the checkpoint verification package and the human decision criteria for each possible checkpoint outcome. The protocol specifies that no checkpoint authorization may be issued verbally: it requires a written digital authorization entry in the construction record.

*Assembly Graph Manager Rerouting Authorization.* The conditions under which rerouting a task from its planned robot to an alternative requires human notification versus human authorization. High-criticality structural tasks: human authorization required. Operational and quality tasks: autonomous rerouting with notification.

*Override Architecture Test Protocol.* Weekly testing of the Emergency Override Architecture's physical independence: verifying that the override channel functions under primary network failure simulation, and that the fleet-wide suspension command reaches all robots within the specified latency. Non-negotiable during active construction operations.

**Layer 3 — Construction Site Infrastructure**
*(Physical systems built into the site from day one)*

Three infrastructure elements are as essential as the robots themselves.

*Site Sensor Network.* A network of fixed sensors — ground-penetrating radar ground-truth stations, atmospheric dust concentration monitors, thermal gradient sensors, structural vibration sensors on completed elements — providing continuous environmental characterization data to the Adaptive Site Model. Not an optional instrument cluster. A permanent infrastructure component that operates independently of any specific robot.

*Emergency Override Physical Plant.* The dedicated radio infrastructure for the Emergency Override Architecture: transmitter towers with independent power, positioned to provide line-of-sight coverage of the full construction zone from at least two independent directions.

*SICI Public Display.* The Structural Integrity Confidence Index displayed continuously in the colony's control center, visible to all engineers and mission leadership, updated at every structural checkpoint. Not an engineering dashboard. A colony-wide instrument as visible as the habitat's atmospheric pressure reading.

