## X. CORE TRAINING ARCHITECTURE

**Phase 1 — Foundation (2 years, integrated academic)**

*Autonomous Systems and Multi-Robot Coordination* — not robotics theory in isolation, but the specific engineering of multi-agent systems operating in partially observable, unstructured environments where communication latency is operationally significant. This includes the theory and practice of dependency graph management, distributed task allocation, and the failure modes of consensus-based coordination under asynchronous communication. The Air France 447 accident report, already identified in the AI Workforce Designer curriculum, remains equally relevant here: the documented mechanics of authority ambiguity between a human crew and an automated system producing catastrophe applies to the Swarm Coherence Protocol's authorization architecture with the same precision it applies to the Decision Topology Specification.

*Structural Mechanics and Reduced-Order Modeling* — the foundation of the SICI framework. Not finite element theory in the abstract, but the specific practice of reduced-order model development for structures whose geometry evolves in real time: how to build a structural model that can be solved at checkpoint frequency, produces certifiable safety factor estimates under the reduced-order approximation, and degrades gracefully when as-built data is incomplete. Includes the practice of structural tolerance analysis — how design tolerances propagate through a construction sequence and compound at structural connections.

*Geomechanics of Planetary Regolith* — the physical basis of the Adaptive Site Model. The mechanics of granular materials under vacuum, low gravity, and extreme thermal cycling. The specific characterization of lunar regolith mechanical behavior from Apollo data and remote sensing, and its known limitations at the scales operationally relevant to construction. The Bayesian updating approach that underlies the Adaptive Site Model's voxel-by-voxel probability distribution maintenance.

*Mechanical Degradation and Prognostic Health Management* — the engineering foundation of the Predictive Degradation Architecture. Dust infiltration mechanisms, thermal cycling fatigue mechanics, radiation-induced degradation of seals and lubricants — studied from laboratory data and from the operational perspective: how prognostic health management systems are designed, what sensor modalities are most informative for specific failure modes, and how remaining useful life distributions are estimated and updated from operational data.

**Phase 2 — Analog Deployment (2 years, operational)**

The practitioner is embedded in the two closest analogs to autonomous robotic construction in extreme environments available on Earth: Antarctic robotic infrastructure deployment programs (where robotic systems operate in extreme cold with minimal human access) and deep-sea robotic installation projects (where remotely operated vehicles work in environments the human operator cannot physically enter, under significant communication latency to surface control, building infrastructure that must be pressure-tight and structurally certified).

In each deployment, the practitioner does not observe the construction program. They operate within it: designing the dependency graph for a specific construction sequence, specifying the Structural Authority Boundary for the robot classes in use, deploying an Adaptive Site Model calibrated to the local environment, and managing the construction sequence through a representative set of Physical Reality Gap events.

The critical experience that no classroom provides: managing a structural checkpoint review in which the as-built photogrammetric survey reveals a deviation that places the SICI in the yellow range — and making the Level 3 decision with full knowledge that the construction timeline does not include a comfortable buffer, with the SICI value on a public display that everyone in the control center is watching. That decision, under that specific pressure, cannot be approximated in a classroom.

And this: watching a robot, during a Deadman Protocol operation, halt at the exact moment the operator releases the switch to respond to an unrelated alert — and understanding viscerally why that architecture is not optional.

**Phase 3 — Lunar-Specific Integration (1 year, simulation)**

Lunar-specific variables modify construction engineering parameters in ways that Earth analogs approximate but do not replicate. The 1.3-second Earth-to-Moon latency eliminates real-time Earth-based support for any time-critical decision: every event requiring faster response than 3–4 seconds must be managed entirely on-site, using only the control interface and the Emergency Override Architecture. The vacuum environment changes the thermal and tribological behavior of every mechanical interface in ways the Antarctic analog only partially captures. The regolith abrasiveness and electrostatic adhesion behavior are uniquely lunar and have no Earth surface analog.

Phase 3 training runs the practitioner through each of the five failure modes in high-fidelity simulation: a Physical Reality Gap Class IV event during a foundation pour; a multi-agent coordination cascade during structural frame assembly; a sudden mechanical failure during a pressure boundary operation; an autonomous mission creep detection that triggers SICI yellowing with a contingency branch decision required under time pressure; and an Emergency Override event under a primary-network fault scenario where the override channel is the only control path available.

The Phase 3 deliverable is a complete construction architecture specification for a defined mission scenario: Adaptive Site Model configuration, Construction Dependency Graph, Structural Authority Boundary taxonomy, Predictive Degradation threshold registry, SICI checkpoint schedule, and Emergency Override Architecture specification for a defined robot fleet, construction sequence, and site environment. This document is the practitioner's capstone and primary professional credential.

