## APPENDIX A: FIVE FAILURE MODES — DETAILED SPECIFICATIONS

### A.1 FAILURE MODE 1 — PHYSICAL REALITY GAP AND THE ADAPTIVE SITE MODEL

**Problem Statement**

Pre-mission models of the construction site cannot accurately characterize regolith mechanical properties, sub-surface composition, and surface microgeometry at centimeter scales required for precision construction. Accumulated divergences between model predictions and physical reality, if undetected, propagate through the construction dependency graph and compound into structural defects. Individual divergences may each be within acceptable limits; their compounded effect at structural connections may not be.

**Architectural Solution: Adaptive Site Model + Physical Reality Gap Protocol + SICI**

| Parameter | Specification |
|---|---|
| Spatial resolution | 10cm × 10cm × 10cm voxel grid |
| Model update trigger | Bayesian posterior update exceeding threshold Δσ |
| Gap classification | Class I–IV based on posterior-to-design-envelope relationship |
| SICI computation | At every structural checkpoint; on-demand after Class III/IV events |
| Human decision: Class III | Within 4 hours of event |
| Human decision: Class IV | Immediately; construction halted until authorization |
| SICI red threshold | SICI ≤ 0 → mandatory construction halt |
| SICI yellow threshold | 0 < SICI ≤ 1 → contingency branch evaluation required |

**Metric for Success**

Zero structural elements installed with SICI below 1.0 at time of installation. Zero Physical Reality Gap Class IV events without documented human Level 3 authorization before construction resumption.

---

### A.2 FAILURE MODE 2 — MULTI-AGENT COORDINATION CASCADE AND THE SWARM COHERENCE PROTOCOL

**Problem Statement**

In a multi-class robot construction fleet, inter-agent task dependencies create the possibility of structural sequence violations when robots make locally reasonable decisions that violate the global dependency graph. Under communication latency, coordination cascades can propagate to multiple structural elements before detection, producing as-built configurations that cannot be corrected without demolition.

**Architectural Solution: Three-Tier Swarm Coherence Protocol + Assembly Graph Manager**

| Parameter | Specification |
|---|---|
| Tier 1 scope | Individual robot; millisecond response; onboard sensors only; no network required |
| Tier 2 scope | Assembly Graph Manager; 1–60 second response; authorization-based task sequencing |
| Tier 3 scope | Structural Checkpoint Authority; human review; mandatory before phase advancement |
| Authorization principle | Robots do not self-authorize next tasks; Assembly Graph Manager issues authorizations |
| SwCI yellow threshold | < 0.95 → Assembly Graph Manager initiates rerouting analysis |
| SwCI red threshold | < 0.85 → all robots suspend; human Level 3 required |
| Tier independence | Tier 1 failure does not propagate to Tier 2; Tier 2 failure does not propagate to physical structure |

**Metric for Success**

Zero structural sequence violations (Category B or C tasks executed before dependency graph prerequisites confirmed). Zero SwCI drops below 0.85 without documented resolution before construction resumption.

---

### A.3 FAILURE MODE 3 — DUST AND THERMAL DEGRADATION CASCADE AND THE PREDICTIVE DEGRADATION ARCHITECTURE

**Problem Statement**

Three simultaneous degradation mechanisms unique to the lunar surface environment — dust infiltration, thermal cycling fatigue, radiation-induced material degradation — interact non-linearly, degrade faster than Earth-analog service histories predict, and produce sudden failure at loads well below rated maximums, creating the risk of robot failure during a structural operation with no prior warning.

**Architectural Solution: Predictive Degradation Architecture + Fleet-Wide Pattern Learning**

| Parameter | Specification |
|---|---|
| Monitoring modalities | Vibration signature analysis, strain gauges, temperature sensors, lubricant viscosity sensors |
| Remaining useful life format | Probability distribution P(failure within T hours) per monitored component |
| δ₁ threshold (priority maintenance) | P(failure < 24h) exceeds mission-phase-calibrated threshold |
| δ₂ threshold (immediate suspension) | P(failure < 4h) exceeds threshold; robot navigated to maintenance area immediately |
| Fleet-wide correlation | Continuous cross-robot analysis for systemic degradation patterns |
| Task allocation constraint | High-criticality tasks routed to highest-health-state available robots |
| Threshold modulation | δ₁, δ₂ adjusted by construction schedule criticality |

**Metric for Success**

Zero mechanical failures during Category B or C operations. Zero robot failures during pressure boundary or foundation grouting tasks. Fleet operational availability above 80% throughout planned construction duration.

---

### A.4 FAILURE MODE 4 — AUTONOMOUS MISSION CREEP AND THE STRUCTURAL AUTHORITY BOUNDARY

**Problem Statement**

Individually reasonable autonomous adjustments to construction parameters, accumulated across hundreds of operations over a construction sequence, produce an as-built structure that has never been analyzed as a system — with compounding deviations from design nominal that may compromise structural integrity under conditions not present in the initial pressurization test.

**Architectural Solution: Structural Authority Boundary Protocol — Three-Category Parameter Taxonomy**

| Category | Scope | Autonomous Authority | Logging | Consequence |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A — Operational | Speed, trajectory, tool selection, intermediate positioning | Yes, within defined ranges | Standard telemetry | None beyond logging |
| B — Quality | Dimensional and material quality within ±50% of design tolerance | Yes | Mandatory: deviation magnitude and compensating action | Automatic SICI recomputation before any dependent task authorized |
| C — Structural | Any parameter requiring structural re-analysis to certify safety | PROHIBITED | N/A — task suspension triggered | Immediate task suspension; human Level 3 decision required |

**Metric for Success**

Zero unauthorized Category C adjustments executed across mission duration. Zero structural elements with undocumented Category B deviations. SICI computed after every Category B deviation event before dependent task authorized.

---

### A.5 FAILURE MODE 5 — HUMAN-ROBOT INTERFACE BREAKDOWN AND THE EMERGENCY OVERRIDE ARCHITECTURE

**Problem Statement**

Under emergency conditions, the primary control interface generates an alert cascade that overwhelms operator situational awareness, while override commands are buried in normal-use navigation. A robot may execute multiple further actions during the time required to reach the override in the primary interface. Primary network faults that precipitate the emergency may simultaneously impair any override channel sharing that network infrastructure.

**Architectural Solution: Emergency Override Architecture + Physical Independence + Deadman Protocol**

| Parameter | Specification |
|---|---|
| Override channel | Dedicated radio link; independent power supply; reserved frequency band |
| Primary interface independence | Override channel fully functional under primary operational network failure |
| Override activation | Single physical switch; no menu navigation; robot-specific or fleet-wide |
| Override latency | Site mesh network latency only; not Earth-Moon latency |
| Minimum viable display | Always-on, always-updated secondary display; no interaction required |
| Deadman Protocol applicability | All Category C parameter operations; all pressure boundary tasks; all foundation grouting |
| Deadman fail-safe | Operator release → immediate robot task suspension regardless of execution stage |
| Test protocol | Weekly during active construction operations; primary network failure simulation required |

**Metric for Success**

Zero failed override commands in any test or operational event. Zero instances of a robot continuing a Category C task following Deadman switch release. Override channel verified functional under primary network failure simulation in every weekly test.

